---
import SlotStates from "../../../components/studies/SlotStates.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "ship-slot-language");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<section aria-labelledby="purpose">
		<h2 id="purpose">01 / A mounting place that explains itself</h2>
		<p>
			A slot is a fixed mounting place with a position, orientation and fitting
			rules. Its overlay explains what fits and which mount is being edited; the
			installed item supplies its own appearance and function. Translucent fills
			keep the hull visible beneath restrained technical markings.
		</p>
		<p>
			Contents and selection form four primary states: empty, filled, selected
			empty and selected filled. Selection means editing in the builder, not an
			engine firing. A linked secondary follows its primary and cannot be
			selected independently. Linking is separate from equipment type.
		</p>
		<p>
			The study covers slot appearance and compatibility language. Hull layout,
			equipment balance and damage mechanics remain outside its scope. Hover and
			keyboard focus accompany the primary states without changing them.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="exploration">
		<h2 id="exploration">02 / Slot language on the Fighter</h2>
		<h3>02.1 / Four states, one hull</h3>
		<p>
			The comparison places four states and a linked pair on production Fighter
			mount centers. Independent slot styling supports comparison while the
			accepted geometry and palette remain fixed. Filled deck examples use the
			same battery symbol to isolate selection from equipment identity.
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Geometry:</strong>
				54 × 64 preview-unit fill, 4-unit gap and 1.5-unit border. Side mounts
				retain their ±26° angles.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Contents:</strong>
				empty slots use 35% acid-lime fill and void-dark borders; filled slots
				reverse those colors. Linked dashed borders and connectors follow the
				same border-color rule.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Markings:</strong>
				empty-slot symbols use the border color at 70% opacity. Numbers use
				ivory on 35% void-dark backing. Vertical type text runs upward from the
				lower-left corner in the border color, without backing. Filled slots
				retain labels but omit the central symbol.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Selection:</strong>
				the accepted baseline uses acid-lime corners with a 5-unit gap and zero
				positional offsets.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<SlotStates client:load />
		<p>
			The accepted reference is 02.1 after vertical type text was added on 31
			October 2026, preserved in SlotStatesAccepted021.svelte. The playground
			uses the subsequently approved Q/Equipment and +/Universal names; the
			original reference used E/W/+ markings. Reset restores the source
			baseline. Temporary browser settings were not captured as decisions.
		</p>
		<p>
			Acceptance covers builder-scale slot language, not placeholder equipment
			artwork, flight readability or fitting behavior. Positional edits remain
			experimental. The type selector changes the preview, not production rules.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="readiness">
		<h2 id="readiness">03 / Builder overlays, equipped items in flight</h2>
		<p>
			Slots belong in the builder. Flight shows hull and equipped items without
			slot fills, borders, selection corners, numbers or connectors. Separate
			overlay geometry preserves physical mount positions, nozzle origins and
			flight forces while allowing the accepted proportions at builder scale.
		</p>
		<p>
			The reference uses SVG; production uses Canvas with accessible HTML hit
			targets. Shared style definitions carry the palette and dimensions across
			that boundary. The comparison contains two selected examples; the real
			builder has one active primary, with keyboard focus distinct from
			selection.
		</p>
		<h3>Approved compatibility mapping</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>Engine (E): engines; used by side mounts.</li>
			<li>Weapon (W): weapons; available without changing chassis layouts.</li>
			<li>Equipment (Q): weapons and utilities; used by hull mounts.</li>
			<li>Universal (+): all part categories; used by aft mounts.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			The left side is primary; the right side is its non-selectable linked
			secondary. The shared side-engine value drives paired fitting. Internal G
			remains the saved part category for guns, separate from slot taxonomy.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="integration">
		<h2 id="integration">04 / Integration and evidence</h2>
		<p>
			The slot contract owns compatibility; buildMounts supplies type and link
			identity. ShipBuilder and /lab/builder share the Canvas renderer and
			accessible hit targets. Builder-only overlays are separate from shared
			hull and item drawing, preserving item bodies and plume origins in flight.
		</p>
		<p>
			The slot integration preserved existing saves. The later engine-catalog
			release deliberately replaced version-1 storage with version 2; the
			original compatibility claim describes the slot change only.
		</p>
		<p>
			The original slot-loop checks and later verification are separate
			observations. The closure record preserves their results and limits.
			Automated checks cover fitting and rendering boundaries; they do not
			establish acceptance of every delivered chassis layout. Human fidelity
			review remains separate.
		</p>
	</section>
</StudyLayout>
